# Terms with a situational meaning in rocksdb-js. PR descriptions define them at first use as <ins title="…">term</ins>.
# One "term: definition" per line; keep each definition to one clause.
env: one Node.js environment, the main thread or a worker_thread, whose teardown must release its native state
DBDescriptor: process-global native state shared by every env opening one database path in the same read-only and secondary mode
DBHandle: one open's native handle onto a descriptor's column family, owned by a single env
tsfn: N-API thread-safe function that delivers native-side completions, events or wakes to its owning env's JS thread
column family: named keyspace within one database, opened as a view by db.use(name) and spanned by transactions and backups
logical drop: column-family drop that is immediate for callers but physical only once no admitted commit names it
transaction snapshot: read view a transaction pins, taken at first read (optimistic) or first read or write (pessimistic)
verification table: process-global hashed slots, each a version or a write lock, that let a cached read skip the get
write intent: lock a transaction write holds on its key's hashed verification-table slot, which colliding keys share
coordinated retry: opt-in mode where a busy-conflicted commit waits, up to a timeout, for the holder's write intent, then reruns
WriteBufferManager stall: writers parked on the manager's own queue, invisible to RocksDB's stall counters and writeStall event
user shared buffer: cross-thread ArrayBuffer keyed by name that lives until its column family closes, never by view lifetime
secondary: read-only follower of a live primary in its own exclusive workspace, advanced by catchUpWithPrimary()
transaction log: append-only .txnlog files of caller-written entries, written just before a transaction's RocksDB commit
segment: one numbered .txnlog sequence file of a log store, superseded by the next when the store rotates
txn.state: side file persisting the flushed position, whose sequence file anchors the retention floor
retention floor: the oldest sequence file an age-based purge must keep, usually the one txn.state's flushed position names
committed position: position of the last transaction committed to RocksDB, where log reads stop unless reading uncommitted
flushed position: log position up to which data has been flushed from memtables to RocksDB's on-disk files
torn tail: partial last write with nothing valid after it, which a writable open erases as the log's true end
mid-log break: corrupt frame with committed entries after it, reported as CorruptFrameError with a resync position
timestamp floor: lower bound the monotonic clock is raised to at open, above every batch key in the timestampFloorLog log
monotonic timestamp: process-wide epoch-millisecond clock made strictly increasing, which issues new transactions' timestamps and log batch keys
steady clock: elapsed-time clock, unrelated to wall time, for durations and deadlines instead of the monotonic timestamp
